gnette even though  it's a super close up photo that may not  even look like dessert but it said  beignet is 92 percent sure it also said  dessert and powdered sugar and then  here's an example of a face detection  response to a picture I took with a  friend so you can see it returns the  result of the emotions it detected we  can see that joy likelihood is very  likely it also returns a landmarks  object which includes a bunch of  different features in your face and XY  coordinates of where those are in the  image so now let's jump into live demo  number two if we could switch to my  computer so I'm going to minimize this  app and I'm going to pull up it's  actually I'm going to stop this from  running I'm going to pull up the bot app  and I'm actually going to have Kathy  come on up here to demonstrate this so  Kathy here has a bot  and I'm going to run this app and we're  going to be able to see what the bot is  looking at so this I believe is bot  number five five seven four and we can  see that she's holding up a flower to it  the lights are throwing it off a little  bit but could be a Christmas decoration  daisy family there we go there's the bot  in action Thank You Kathy that was  awesome  so this is what the app is doing it's  got a list of all the active BOTS I can  click on them and then I can see what  they're looking at so that's kind of an  overview of what the app does now I want  to walk you through the codes that you  can see a little bit more complex app  built with firebase so first I want to  show you the table view controller so  here we set up our firebase reference  I've got a helper class to do that and  we want to have a variable with the bot  I D because we're going to use this when  we segue from the table view controller  to the detail view controller we want to  pass that bot ID so that we can create  our new firebase reference and then we  also have our local bot array of F data  snapshots so the first thing we want to  do is in viewed 